Benin: Full text of the communique of the Council of Ministers held in Porto-Novo

The Council of Ministers met in ordinary session on Wednesday 23 April 2014 in Porto-Novo under the chairmanship of the President of the Republic, Head of State, Head of Government.

At the beginning of the session, the Head of State observed a minute of silence in memory of the late:
- Yvette Angele Felicite TITUS, wife of DAIZO, mother of Mrs Ginette Ernestine DAIZO, Departmental Director of Culture, Literacy, Handicrafts and Tourism for Atlantique and Littoral;
- Daate MANTE TCHOROPA, war veteran, father of Mr Louis Yatopa MANTE, Labour Inspector, former Sub-Prefect;
- Ignacio Koladjo ODOUBOUROU, Head of the Statistical Studies and Performance Service at the General Directorate of the Autonomous Port of Cotonou;
- Daniel NAGNONHOU, father-in-law of Mr Justin A. SOSSOU, Deputy Secretary General of the Ministry of Health;
- Barthelemy NATO, Founder of the Pentecostal Church of Faith.

During the session, the Council of Ministers adopted several draft decrees, namely:
- a draft decree ending the secondment to the Constitutional Court of Mrs Clemence YIMBERE, wife of DANSOU, Magistrate;
- a draft decree ending the secondment to the Constitutional Court of Mrs Clotilde MEDEGAN, wife of NOUGBODE, Magistrate;
- a draft decree creating the Office for the Management of World Food Programme Projects (OGP-PAM) and approving its statutes;
- a draft decree creating the revenue-collection offices of the communes of Karimama, Lalo and Agbangnizoun;
- a draft decree setting the conditions for the conduct of the 2013-2014 cashew nut marketing campaign;
- a draft decree on the creation, powers, organisation and functioning of the National Institute for Training Engineering and Capacity Building of Trainers (INIFRCF);
- a draft decree amending decree no. 2010-021 of 4 February 2010 appointing the members of the National Commission for Information Technology and Liberties;
- a draft decree on the implementing arrangements for law no. 2012-36 of 15 February 2013 on the creation of the Beninese Human Rights Commission;
- the draft implementing decrees for law no. 2013-06 of 25 November 2013 establishing the electoral code of the Republic of Benin, notably:
  - the decree setting the form and content of campaign accounts for presidential, legislative, communal or municipal elections and for members of village or city-district councils;
  - the decree on the arrangements for drawing up and signing the minutes compiling the results of the ballot by district (arrondissement);
  - the decree on the arrangements for designating the district coordinator;
  - the decree on the arrangements for keeping the register of minutes of the first centralisation of sealed envelopes by the district coordinator; the decree on the arrangements for drawing up and signing the minutes compiling the results of the ballot by village or city district.

During the session, the Council of Ministers examined several communications, notably:
- a communication from the Minister of Economy and Finance on the state of implementation of the decisions of the Council of Ministers for the first half of 2013;
- a communication from the Minister of Health on the payment of Benin's arrears of contributions to the African Public Health Emergency Fund for the 2012-2013 biennium.

The Council of Ministers also approved several reports, including:
- a report by the Minister of the Interior, Public Security and Religious Affairs on the 37th session of the joint Benin-Togo border commission, held in Bassila, Ouake, Savalou, Djidja and Djakotomey from 3 to 5 February 2014;
- a report by the Minister of Decentralisation, Local Governance, Administration and Territorial Planning on the operation to have the national budget take over the salaries of the communications staff of the former urban districts and sub-prefectures;
- a joint report by the Minister of Development, Economic Analysis and Forecasting and the Minister of Industry, Trade and Small and Medium Enterprises on the 12th AGOA forum, held in Addis Ababa, Ethiopia, from 9 to 13 August 2013;
- a joint report by the Minister of Economy and Finance and the Minister of Energy, Petroleum and Mineral Research, Water and Renewable Energy Development on the official visit of a delegation of the Ministers in charge of Finance and Energy in the framework of negotiations for the financing and execution of the Adjaralla hydroelectric development works on the Mono River, in China, from 18 to 25 November 2013;
- a report by the Minister of Labour, Public Service, Administrative and Institutional Reform, in charge of Social Dialogue, on the proceedings of the first extraordinary session of the National Labour Council (CNT) on raising the Guaranteed Interprofessional Minimum Wage (SMIG);
- a report by the Minister of Urban Planning, Housing and Sanitation on the preparation of the Urban Development and Decentralisation Support Project (PAURAD). On this occasion the Minister of Urban Planning, Housing and Sanitation also gave the weekly update on the communal sanitation programme financed by the BOAD and the BIDC.

It emerges from the summary table of the projects presented that the works concern: the paving and drainage of streets, for the most part carried out by AGETUR SA, AGETIP BENIN SA and SERHAU SA with financing from the national budget, the BOAD and the BIDC, over a total length exceeding 60,000 linear metres; urban development and support for decentralisation (PAURAD) financed by the IDA, involving about ten communes. In approving this report, the Council of Ministers decided to include the communes of Zogbodome and Ze in the sanitation projects financed by the BOAD and those of Malanville and Adjohoun under the BIDC projects. Instructions were given in this context to the Minister in charge of Urban Planning to travel on mission to the BIDC and the BOAD in Lome to follow up the progress of these important projects and to obtain the lifting of the no-objection notices so as to proceed in the near future with the launch of works in about ten of our communes in order to improve the living environment of our hard-working populations. The Minister of Urban Planning, Housing and Sanitation also reported on the progress of the process that led to the selection of a firm to assess the quality of the works on the construction site of the new headquarters of the National Assembly. The activities of the selected firm will begin as soon as possible.
- a report on the information mission on the introduction of animal-traction equipment from 2 to 6 September 2013 in Burkina Faso;
- a report on the reflection workshop on the organisation and functioning of the CARDER. In examining this communication, the Council decided to hold a governmental seminar to look more deeply into this matter;
- a report by the Minister of Public Works and Transport on Benin's participation in the meeting of the steering committee of the project for the development and construction of the Abidjan-Lagos corridor motorway, held in Accra from 17 to 20 September 2013.

The Minister of Health reported to the Council of Ministers on the visit made last weekend by the President of the Republic to the construction site of the Djidja Zone Hospital. The Council of Ministers instructed the Minister of Health to: invite the contracting authority AGETIP to take appropriate steps to lift the reservations recorded on the structure within a maximum of two (02) weeks; raise the awareness of the Communal Council with a view to ensuring the cleanliness of the hospital through regular cleaning. The Minister of Economy and Finance was also instructed to have prompt arrangements made to release the equipment supplier's material in order to speed up the equipping of the hospital within two (02) months.

Furthermore, the Secretary General of the Presidency of the Republic reported to the Council of Ministers on the work plan of the Special Adviser to the Presidency of the Republic in charge of Governance of Public Enterprises and Internal Control. It emerges from this report that the work plan will make it possible to: ensure that all the managers of the eight (08) public enterprises have taken note of the recommendations of the latest management audits and that they take adequate measures to implement those recommendations; the need to train all members of the Boards of Directors, after their recruitment, on the roles and functioning of a Board of Directors and the principles of governance of a Board of Directors; the need to have all Public Enterprises develop or correct their internal control system after its evaluation; the need to draft internal procedure manuals in collaboration with all Public Enterprises; the need to organise a forum of the Directors General of Benin, during which the list of the 50 best enterprises will be published; the need to have the mission evaluated at the end of six months by an independent team.

In approving this work plan, the Council of Ministers decided to open a new page in the life of public enterprises in our country through the introduction of a new corporate governance that benefits the productive sector and thus contributes to the competitiveness of the national economy. With this in mind, instructions were given to the Special Adviser to the President of the Republic for Governance of Public Enterprises and Internal Control, in liaison with the Minister of Economy and Finance, to present to the next Council of Ministers an adjusted road map making it possible to: define a policy and a strategy for the sound management of the said enterprises; clarify the missions of the said enterprises; define internal and external control and audit mechanisms; define the management and governing bodies as well as their mode of governance; determine the arrangements for recruiting the members of the Board of Directors and senior managers who combine competence with ethics and morality; draw up mission letters with obligations as to results.

Still within the framework of governance, the Council of Ministers reiterated its decision to have the accounts of the ORTB audited under the programme for full coverage of the national territory, as well as those of the SBEE. These audits, for which decrees have been issued, should make it possible to obtain a clear picture, notably of the uses to which the resources employed in these enterprises have been put.

The Minister of Agriculture, Livestock and Fisheries and the Minister of Development, Economic Analysis and Forecasting reported to the Council of Ministers on the state of agricultural statistics in Benin. Indeed, as part of the initiatives to strengthen capacities in agricultural statistics in Benin with a view to improving agricultural statistics, the Focal Unit of MCA-Benin, in liaison with the Ministry of Agriculture, Livestock and Fisheries, requested from the Millennium Challenge Corporation (MCC) financing for an in-depth evaluation of the agricultural statistics system in Benin. This evaluation, which will take place from 12 to 23 May 2014 by an MCC mission, will provide a first answer to the problem of the collection and management of statistics on agriculture and food security in the framework of the formulation of Benin's 2nd compact. In approving this communication, the Council of Ministers instructed the Minister of Development, Economic Analysis and Forecasting and the Minister of Agriculture, Livestock and Fisheries: 1. to put in place governance structures or entrust existing structures with the implementation and monitoring of the initiative to improve agricultural statistics; 2. to draw up a road map of the activities to be carried out before the arrival of the mission; 3. to organise meetings with the identified structures.

The Minister at the Presidency of the Republic in charge of coordinating the Policies for the implementation of the Millennium Development Goals and the Sustainable Development Goals reported to the Council on the progress of the process of implementing the DOING BUSINESS measures. In approving this report, the Council of Ministers instructed the Minister in charge of the MDGs and SDGs, in liaison with the Minister of Communication and Information and Communication Technologies, to prepare a communication plan to enable the Ministers concerned to report on the improvement of the business environment in their respective sectors on local and international communication channels as well as towards the diplomatic missions.

The Minister in charge of Relations with Institutions reported to the Council of Ministers on the financial resources requested by the COS LEPI in the framework of the process of updating the national electoral register and the Permanent Computerised Electoral List (LEPI). In approving this communication, the Council instructed the Minister of Economy and Finance: to make available to the COS LEPI the sum of two billion CFA francs, bringing the total disbursements for the benefit of the COS-LEPI to six billion one hundred and eleven million nine hundred and forty-three thousand nine hundred and thirteen (6,111,943,913) CFA francs with a view to carrying out the process; to report on this to the next Council of Ministers.

The Council of Ministers further authorised: the travel of a delegation of the Ministry of Secondary Education, Technical and Vocational Training, Retraining and Youth Integration to Quebec; the organisation of the international conference by the Minister of Energy, Petroleum and Mineral Research, Water and Renewable Energy Development from 24 to 26 April 2014 in Cotonou on the theme "Energy, Petroleum and Mineral Resources: Driver of Sustainable Development in Benin"; the organisation of the Civil Society Organisations days in Lokossa, from 17 to 22 October 2014; the official installation of the Rector of the Polytechnic University of Abomey; the participation of the Chief of the General Staff of the Beninese Armed Forces in the seminar on maritime security in the Gulf of Guinea on 7 and 8 April 2014 in Abidjan in the Republic of Cote d'Ivoire; the participation of a delegation of the Beninese Armed Forces in the ceremony for the award of the United Nations medal to the members of the Beninese contingent of the United Nations Operation in Cote d'Ivoire (ONUCI) from 8 to 11 May 2014; the signing of contracts with NGOs in the framework of the implementation of the project "10 million souls, 10 million trees" at commune level in liaison with the Interministerial Committee set up to monitor this project; the holding of the 3rd session of the Grand Joint Commission for Benin-Ivorian Cooperation in Cotonou on 2 and 3 May 2014; the medical assessment of air force flight personnel for the year 2014.

The Council of Ministers also approved: a communication from the Minister of State in charge of Higher Education and Scientific Research on the approval of the financing method and procurement procedure for twenty (20) buses for student transport for the benefit of the Centre for University and Social Works of Abomey-Calavi (COUS-AC) and the new university centres; a communication from the Minister of National Defence on the national budget assuming responsibility for the supply of clothing for the military personnel of the Beninese Armed Forces; a communication from the Secretary General of the Presidency of the Republic on making available the resources needed for the mission of the Special Adviser to the Presidency of the Republic in charge of Governance of Public Enterprises and Internal Control; a joint communication from the Minister of Agriculture, Livestock and Fisheries and the Minister in charge of Microfinance, Youth and Women's Employment on the authorisation for the full release of the credit component fund of the Milk and Meat Sectors Support Project (PAFILAV) for the benefit of the National Microfinance Fund; six communications from the Minister of Agriculture, Livestock and Fisheries relating successively to: the harmonisation of the floor price and purchase price of certified seeds at national level; the arrangements to improve the level of implementation of the Milk and Meat Sectors Support Project (PAFILAV); the arrangements for receiving GIROLANDO cattle and for encouraging their acquisition by breeders; the acquisition of additional packaging for the conditioning of cotton fibre and oil seeds for the 2013-2014 campaign; additional urgent measures for destocking and moving seed cotton from warehouses and storage sites to ginning factories for the 2013-2014 cotton campaign; and the inventory of tractors and other agricultural equipment under PPMA management throughout the territory; a communication from the Minister in charge of the Environment on the authorisation to sign a direct-award contract with the military engineering corps in the framework of the development of lowlands and the construction of water mobilisation structures in the PANA project demonstration villages of Lagbave, Dame (Aplahoue), Kadolassi (Ouake) and Kankini-Seri (Materi); a joint communication from the Minister of the Interior, Public Security and Religious Affairs, the Minister of National Defence and the Minister of Public Works and Transport on the Governmental tour for the implementation of decree no. 2013-546 of 24 December 2013 regulating road and security checkpoints on the corridors in the Republic of Benin.

This tour is in application of the UEMOA directives on the practical arrangements for implementing the regional control plan on inter-State roads and of decree no. 2013-546 of 24 December 2013 regulating road and security checkpoints on the corridors in the Republic of Benin, which set the roads concerned and the number of checkpoints per road. These are: 1. the Cotonou-Malanville road (03 checkpoints); 2. the Cotonou-Porga road (03 checkpoints); 3. the Cotonou-Hillacondji road (1 checkpoint); 4. the Cotonou-Krake road (1 checkpoint); 5. the Cotonou-Igolo road (1 checkpoint).

The units responsible for these security checks and road checks of goods, persons and property, namely the National Police, Customs, the National Gendarmerie, Water and Forestry, and the health, phytosanitary and zoosanitary control services, are obliged to scrupulously observe these new requirements, which aim to make our roads vectors of development and not nests of extortion and corruption. Consequently, it is formally forbidden for all these control units: to erect, without authorisation, physical barriers or chicanes on the roads (devices causing vehicles to tip); to collect improper charges at authorised road and security checkpoints; to authorise any union control or any collection of union tax on the corridors concerned.

Agents of the road control and security structures on mission on the said corridors are required to identify themselves clearly by properly wearing their name badge clearly showing their surname and first names, their registration number, their armband and their mission order. Any control agent contravening the requirements recalled above will be liable to disciplinary sanctions which may go as far as dismissal, without prejudice to criminal sanctions where appropriate. The law will be applied rigorously and without any leniency.

The Minister of the Maritime Economy and Port Infrastructure reported to the Council on the tariffs charged at the port of Cotonou to hauliers for the entry and exit of trucks. It emerges from this report that, despite the setting of a single tariff of ten thousand (10,000) francs for the entry and exit of trucks, it turned out that the acting Director General of the Autonomous Port of Cotonou had continued to charge hauliers an additional amount, not paid over to the single window, of five thousand nine hundred (5,900) CFA francs, for badge (macaron) fees. This brings to fifteen thousand nine hundred (15,900) the entry and exit fees per truck, in disregard of the decisions of the Council of Ministers and of the reminders issued. This situation provoked a reaction from the hauliers, who on Monday 14 April 2014 refused to let trucks into the Port, blocking access number 5. This behaviour is contrary to the Government's policy of making the Port of Cotonou more competitive every day by eliminating improper charges and ensuring the traceability of all revenue through the single window. Taking note of this report, the Council of Ministers decided to remove Mr KASSIM TRAORE from his functions as acting Director General of the Autonomous Port of Cotonou.

At the end of the proceedings of the Council of Ministers, the members of the Government went to the sites of the ADJINA amusement park currently being built with BOAD financing and of the RING ROAD (ROCADE), whose construction will begin in June 2014.

